        "content": "<p>Hello!</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1205353\">Paul Licameli</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629403596\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How can I make this more elegant or idiomatic?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Are you asking how to golf your proofs, or what an idiomatic version looks like?<br>\nGenerally: Avoid using tactics to create data, unbundling is nicer (define a predicate on arrays instead of a new type), and <code>Array</code>s are usually meant for programming; use <code>List</code> instead.</p>\n<p>Batteries has the nice <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=List.IsChain.cons_of_ne_nil#doc\">docs#List.IsChain.cons_of_ne_nil</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=List.IsChain.of_cons#doc\">docs#List.IsChain.of_cons</a>, which are a more idiomatic version of your <code>Chain.push</code> and <code>Chain.pop</code> in my opinion.</p>",
        "id": 629407800,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791253116
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629407800\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Hello!</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1205353\">Paul Licameli</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629403596\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How can I make this more elegant or idiomatic?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Are you asking how to golf your proofs, or what an idiomatic version looks like?<br>\nGenerally: Avoid using tactics to create data, unbundling is nicer (define a predicate on arrays instead of a new type), and <code>Array</code>s are usually meant for programming; use <code>List</code> instead.</p>\n<p>Batteries has the nice <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=List.IsChain.cons_of_ne_nil#doc\">docs#List.IsChain.cons_of_ne_nil</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=List.IsChain.of_cons#doc\">docs#List.IsChain.of_cons</a>, which are a more idiomatic version of your <code>Chain.push</code> and <code>Chain.pop</code> in my opinion.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Thanks for the advice.  Batteries is a library, then, of \"wheels\" like this, already invented?</p>\n<p>This morning, another related problem occurred to me:  How can you define an iterator with a proof that all adjacent pairs it produces are related?  And even for potentially infinite sequences?</p>\n<p>In my example, most of the lines of code are proofs that invariants are established and maintained, so what's wrong with tactics?</p>\n<p>And what's wrong with programming :-) ?  Lean has its dual use as a programming language too.  But a language where strong proofs of correctness properties of the program can also be carried in the code, making much unit testing unnecessary.  I'm tring to explore the possibilities and get acquainted with techniques.</p>\n<p>I was a strong believer in programming by contract in my C++ days, but still at best that was a certain discipline of conventional comments and proper usages of <code>assert</code>, whereas proof-carrying code was what I wished I really had -- that \"assertions\" were really things proven and not places where the program would panic if human reasonings had been incomplete.</p>\n<p>Bundling a structure with its important invariants seems to me, a very good idea, if you are trying to build and maintain large programs and have high confidence of correctness.  Use types to greatest advantage.  You lose part of that advantage if you \"unbundle\" and work too much at the lower level of abstraction.</p>",
        "id": 629529791,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Licameli",
        "timestamp": 1791290269
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629407800\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Hello!</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1205353\">Paul Licameli</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629403596\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How can I make this more elegant or idiomatic?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Are you asking how to golf your proofs</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>\"Golf\"?  What?</p>",
        "id": 629580961,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Licameli",
        "timestamp": 1791300308
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The term golf is sometimes used in a programming context, e.g. \"Code golf\", a competition where you aim to accomplish some task in the fewest bytes possible.</p>",
        "id": 629583168,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1791300735
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Taking this to an extreme can result in worse proofs (in terms of tradeoffs with maintainability and readability), but when applied in moderation to overly long first drafts, it make make you think more carefully about the structure of a proof.</p>",
        "id": 629583790,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1791300871
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"687698\">Chris Henson</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629583168\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The term golf is sometimes used in a programming context, e.g. \"Code golf\", a competition where you aim to accomplish some task in the fewest bytes possible.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ah yes, sink it with fewest (key) strokes.</p>\n<p>Well I like density of ideas in a screenful of code, without obscurity.  Where that line lies is a matter of taste.</p>\n<p>I see I might simplify some of my previous (and I add one more):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pop</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elems</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">-- show invariant preservation</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">intros</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">repeat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Array</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">getElem_pop</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">invariant</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EmptyCollection</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">emptyCollection</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intros</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">contradiction</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">singleton</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">relation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Chain</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">contradiction</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>And I limit the scope of <code>by</code> to the construction of propositions, only, and I avoid needless intro bindings.  These look more pleasing now.</p>\n<p>Still I wonder how I could make the very long proof in <code>push</code> nicer.</p>",
        "id": 629612473,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Licameli",
        "timestamp": 1791307231
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1205353\">Paul Licameli</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629529791\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Batteries is a library, then, of \"wheels\" like this, already invented?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>it's \"batteries\" as in \"lean with batteries included\"</p>",
        "id": 629629213,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Waring",
        "timestamp": 1791311081
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This \"batteries\" idiom (while not originating from programming) also appears in other languages like OCaml and Python</p>",
        "id": 629631101,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1791311513
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>Still I wonder how I could make the very long proof in <code>push</code> nicer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>in the case where <code>i + 1 = c.elems.size</code> you can use <code>grind</code> to solve it in one line</p>",
        "id": 629700415,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1791332485
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"380294\">Matt Diamond</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20structure.20.20invariant.20preservation.3A.20.20simplify.20this.3F/near/629700415\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Still I wonder how I could make the very long proof in <code>push</code> nicer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>in the case where <code>i + 1 = c.elems.size</code> you can use <code>grind</code> to solve it in one line</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Cool, but now I'm unhappy that I don't get it why this big hammer  called <code>grind</code> works in this case but not others.</p>",
        "id": 629932814,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Licameli",
        "timestamp": 1791397250
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>tauto</code>, <code>simp</code>, <code>grind</code>, <code>linarith</code>, <code>assumption</code>, <code>contradiction</code>, <code>omega</code>, ...</p>\n<p>Many tools in the box to shorten proofs.  Some work sometimes, some don't.  I don't yet get the nuances.</p>\n<p>And as a matter of style and taste, to write a proof that is understandable not just to a machine but also to a human reader versed in the notation -- where's the line between too much and too little abbreviation?</p>\n<p>Also if one ever develops a large programming project in Lean (not just a proof library), compilation times might matter and overreliance on <code>grind</code> might be a bad thing, right?</p>\n<p>What are the most noteworthy examples of large programming projects in Lean?</p>",
        "id": 629935377,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Licameli",
        "timestamp": 1791397904
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In my learning projects, I’ve been treating <code>grind</code> like <code>sorry</code>, stuff I come back to after I’ve figured out how to translate my ideas into lean. As I’ve gotten more comfortable with lean proofs, I’ve been more willing to let <code>grind</code> handle the tedious bits that aren’t about the math. I’m also curious, though, about the performance impact of its use.</p>",
        "id": 630780418,
        "sender_full_name": "aerh",
        "timestamp": 1791739603
    }
]